sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design (proposed 2026-11-06): the task image is built with the same Ansible phases as Nodes (node:converge), reading the pins of recipe.json; no orbit-agent in the build or in the image identity. Runs only for a new host or a new base OS; every release afterwards refreshes the image by converge (stage 6), using the same phases.
  %% status: reviewed
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the build never promotes an image without a seed for it. Without a seed it stops before the promote and keeps the candidate; the stage 6 refresh promotes the image together with its seed. Promoting seedless images broke all task VM provisioning (task_vm.base_image_missing).
  %% command: none · task image build for a task VM host
  %% kind: task_vm.image_build
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane H icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  %% lane B icon=server pill=builder tone=purple
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant H as Task VM host<br/>Incus over ssh
  participant B as Builder VM<br/>stock Ubuntu
  Note over GW: Admit the build<br/>new host · new base OS
  %%> When:
  %%> - an active task VM host with no image yet, or a new base OS in recipe.json; never per release (stage 6 converges instead)
  %%> - `php artisan task-vms:build-image HOST`
  %%> Result:
  %%> - full intent recorded: the host SSH target with host keys, the builder and smoke names, the base OS, the release commit whose playbooks and pins apply
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-image-build
  SW->>H: Remove earlier attempts<br/>forEach builder · smoke
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one `incus delete --force` per name, through `@swamp/ssh exec`; a missing instance is fine
  SW->>H: Launch the builder<br/>stock image · cloud-init key
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `incus launch` of the stock Ubuntu image of the base OS as a VM, with cloud-init that creates the `orbit` account with 209's key
  %%> - no orbit-agent: the observer is installed at enrolment, not in the image
  SW->>H: Wait for the builder<br/>incus agent · cloud-init done
  SW->>B: Run the task image phases<br/>Ansible · same as Nodes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Swamp on 209 runs `ansible-playbook` over SSH to the builder through the host (ProxyJump with the recorded host keys), as node:converge reaches a Node
  %%> - the phases of the task VM role, in order: software (apt sources and packages, JavaScript, Rust, Pi), tools (the shape-gate linters, the NFS server), check
  %%> - versions come from recipe.json of the release commit; the same playbooks converge a running task VM in stage 2 and the refresh VM in stage 6
  %%> Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06), the package cache of the task VM:
  %%> - the first software task installs stock `apt-cacher-ng` from the VM's own sources (not in the recipe: it is not a pinned tool) and starts it; a drop-in keeps `.deb` files out of its cache and serves the files of a snapshot without asking the snapshot service again (a snapshot never changes, and its answers to a revalidation fail now and then)
  %%> - the phases then read the Ubuntu snapshot through it (`http://snapshot.ubuntu.com` with the apt proxy `localhost:3142`; apt still checks the signatures), so the build itself fills the cache with the snapshot indexes
  %%> - the cache stays in the image: every task VM starts with the indexes of the recipe's snapshot, and the nested Nodes of its fleet read them from the VM (fleet build, `Add app-dev and app-prod`)
  %%> - the end check reads that the cache is active
  %%> Why:
  %%> - measured 2026-11-06 (fleet build, VM 714): each nested Node spent 11 min (app-dev) and 4 min (app-prod) refreshing 30 MB of indexes from snapshot.ubuntu.com; from a warm cache the same refresh takes about 30 s
  SW->>H: Clean and stop<br/>cloud-init · host keys
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `cloud-init clean`, an empty machine-id, removed SSH host keys, then stop; each one `@swamp/ssh exec` argv
  SW->>H: Publish the candidate<br/>incus publish
  SW->>H: Smoke the candidate<br/>boot a copy · check phase
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - boots a throwaway VM from the candidate and runs the Ansible check phase against it, then removes it
  SW->>H: Check the seed<br/>topology seed of the candidate
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review: lists the volumes of the host's pool and looks for `orbit-topology-seed-<first 12 hex of the fingerprint>`
  %%> - the next step runs only when that seed exists
  SW->>H: Promote the image<br/>host lock · keep -previous
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - moves the host's task image alias to the candidate; the old image stays as `-previous`, the rollback copy
  %%> - skipped when the candidate has no seed
  SW->>H: Remove builder and smoke<br/>always
  opt the candidate has no topology seed
    Note over SW,H: Promote with its seed<br/>stage 6 · save copies
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - the stage 6 refresh builds the seed for the new image and promotes the image together with it
    %%> Not designed:
    %%> - building a nested fleet from scratch, without ready copies, for a host with no seed at all; the old builder was deleted (#2767)
  end
  alt the candidate has a seed and every step succeeds
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>image fingerprint
    Note over GW: Record the image<br/>claims use it
  else the candidate has no seed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>candidate kept, not promoted
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the candidate and its fingerprint are recorded without a promotion; claims keep the promoted image
  else a step fails
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the promoted image stays in service; the next build starts from a stock image again
  end
